Response to the team

Verdict: accept the critique, and choose Candidate C, Enterprise Learning Intelligence, with Candidate B as its engine and Candidate A deferred.

I should be open about one thing. C is largely the problem-thread idea I proposed two rounds ago, with remediation themes and time added. I dropped threads when they were called “too descriptive”. I am reversing because the team has now given a reason I had not weighed: repeated occurrence and repeated remediation are directly observable, while effectiveness is not. That is an argument about what the data can support, and it is better than the earlier “too small” objection. It also means the intervention study’s own caveats were pointing at the answer.

Where the team is right
The “compared to what” problem is fatal for A as a lead product. Its most interesting result (for example “training shows no durable change”) is the one it cannot defend. The design needed a matched comparison, exposure data we may not have, and a no-causal-claims rule that then empties the headline.
Finding B is easier to act on than Finding A. It is a statement about the organisation’s structure, and it needs no counterfactual.
Where I would tighten the team’s framing
“Directly observable” is too strong. Records are observable. That two records describe the same mechanism is model-derived and needs expert confirmation. Using “observable” loosely would repeat the earlier mistake of treating constructed patterns as facts.
“Continue to appear despite repeated attention” smuggles a causal claim back in. “Despite” implies the attention should have worked. Use “alongside”: the family has had issues and actions recorded, and incidents continued to be recorded.
“Never been owned or treated as an enterprise problem” cannot be read off the records. What the data can show is that no single issue or action set spans the business units where the family appears. Anything about ownership of people stays out, in line with the guardrails.
“311 actions” is a count of records, not effort. Action granularity varies by team practice, and the schema list shows no cost or effort field. Report counts as counts, and ask whether a cost or effort field exists.
Summed impact is dominated by a few events. Show the severe tail separately, as with the earlier lanes.
