Why C beats B: B stops at the failure side. Its output is “this mechanism has this trajectory”, and the executive response is “interesting”. C adds what the organisation did across issues and actions, which is what makes a funding or ownership decision possible.

Why C beats A: it does not depend on the contested causal step. It is also a prerequisite for A, since mechanism tagging, response classification and family assembly are the first three steps of both. If C works and a later question needs it, A becomes a gated extension, rather than a bet made first.

The capability

Enterprise Learning Intelligence. For each recurring failure family over ten-plus years: what failed (mechanism), how the organisation named it (issue themes), how it responded (remediation themes), where it appeared (process, application, product, business unit), and when.

Unit of analysis: the problem family, a validated grouping of incidents and issues sharing a mechanism, with its actions and context attached. Mechanisms from incidents and weaknesses from issues stay separate vocabularies, and the link between them is a hypothesis for experts to confirm.

What a family shows, all descriptive:

Consequence in the three lanes, not summed
Spread across years, units, products and systems
Record counts of issues and actions, and the mix of remediation themes
Timing: first recorded incident, first issue, first response, and gaps between them (as distributions)
Whether any single issue or action set spans the units where the family appears

Controls: kept out of the first release. Repeated control weakness themes can later be read from issue narratives and control-gap detail, which does not need ratings or control tables.

An illustrative slide (placeholders only): “One failure family: [N] issues, [M] actions and [K] incidents across [J] business units over [Y] years. [X] of the issues use different root cause labels. No single issue spans the units. Consequence: [lane figures].”

