The two-week kill test

The key design change is seed by family, not by domain. The value is cross-unit fragmentation, and a single-domain pilot would hide it.

The sponsor and two experts name about 10 candidate families (for example duplicate payments) and write down what they expect to find, including how many units and years each spans. This is the surprise baseline.
Build each family two ways: from recorded Cross Ref links plus shared root cause labels, and from text meaning across incidents, issues and actions.
Two experts independently judge “same problem or not” on a sample. Pre-agree a minimum agreement (proposal: kappa or alpha of at least 0.6).
Measure the gap between the text method and the label-and-link baseline, and the gap between the result and the sponsor’s prediction.

Proposed pass (to agree before the run): at least five families, each with three or more issues across two or more business units, connected by neither a recorded link nor a shared label, confirmed by both experts, with at least some results the sponsor did not predict.

Stop:

The baseline finds most of what the text method finds.
Experts cannot agree on what counts as one problem.
The sponsor’s predictions are matched, or the sponsor would change nothing under any outcome.
Risks
Similarity is not sameness, and repeated issues may be legitimate (different systems or exposures). Families are hypotheses until experts confirm them, and the output flags candidates, not duplication.
Record counts reflect practice. Better detection, issue-raising culture and action splitting all inflate counts. Reporting indicators (logging lag, low-impact share, near-miss share) accompany every family.
Left-censored history understates duration and latency. Show observation windows.
Business unit data: I do not see a business unit column in the named incident fields. The “across 14 business units” finding depends on one existing and being resolvable. Please confirm.
No causal or ranking claims. Prohibited wording includes “failed”, “worked”, “despite”, “caused”, “ineffective”. No owner, team or business unit comparisons. Business units appear as spread, not performance.
Heavy tail: severe events shown separately.
What carries over and what changes
Carries over: profiling checks (snapshots, Cross Ref reconciliation, text quality and leakage, entity resolution, eras, privacy, tokens), the evidence classes, the lanes, and the incident-first mapping.
Changes: the pilot is a seeded set of families across domains, not one domain.
Shelved: LLAC and period modelling, action-set mapping, eligibility routes, the review queue. The intervention response study stays on file as a gated follow-on if confirmed families show enough episodes.

Next step: if the team agrees, I would draft the two-week kill test specification: the seed-family selection method, the baseline construction, the expert “same problem” labelling guide and the sponsor prediction template. One question first: who is the sponsor who would write the predictions and take a confirmed family to the planning cycle?
